==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/bɹɪt/}}
* {{rhymes|ɪt}}

===Etymology 1===
From {{etyl|enm}} {{term|brytten|lang=enm}}, {{term|brutten|lang=enm}}, from {{etyl|ang}} {{term|brittian|lang=ang}}, {{term|bryttian||to divide, dispense, distribute, rule over, possess, enjoy the use of|lang=ang}}, from {{proto|Germanic|brutjanan|to break, divide|lang=en}}, from {{proto|Indo-European|bʰreud-|to break|lang=en}}. Cognate with {{etyl|is|-}} {{term|brytja||to chop up, break in pieces, slaughter|lang=is}}, {{etyl|sv|-}} {{term|bryta||to break, fracture, cut off|lang=sv}}, {{etyl|da|-}} {{term|bryde||to break|lang=da}}. Related to {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|brytta||dispenser, giver, author, governor, prince|lang=ang}}, {{etyl|ang|-}} {{term|breotan|brēotan|to break in pieces, hew down, demolish, destroy, kill|lang=ang}}.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|britt}}
* {{l|en|brite}} {{qualifier|dialectal}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb|britt|ing}}

# {{transitive}} To [[break]] in pieces; [[divide]].
# {{transitive}} To [[bruise]]; [[indent]].
# {{intransitive}} To [[fall]] out or [[shatter]] (as overripe hops or grain).
# {{intransitive|dialectal}} To [[fade]] away; [[alter]].

=====Derived terms=====
* {{l|en|britten}}
* {{l|en|brittle}}

===Etymology 2===
Probably from {{term|bret|lang=en}} or {{term|birt|lang=enm}}, applied to a different kind of fish. See {{l|en|bret}}.

